(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Electronics I (Semiconductor Devices)"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Electronics I (Semiconductor Devices)"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Electronics I (Semiconductor Devices): Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nUse V_T = 25.85 mV, n_i = 1.5 × 10^10 cm^-3 and q = 1.602 × 10^-19 C unless stated. Show the model you are using (ideal, 0.7 V drop, exponential, small-signal), the region of operation of each device and the check that confirms it. All numbers below were computed and checked by solving the equations numerically.\n\n# Assignment 1: Semiconductors, Diodes and Rectifiers (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Semiconductor physics (10 marks)** (a) In n-type silicon with N_D = 5 × 10^15 cm^-3 find the hole concentration at 300 K and the resistivity (μ_n = 1350 cm²/(V s)) [4]. (b) Find the built-in potential of a pn junction with N_A = 10^17 and N_D = 10^16 cm^-3 [3]. (c) Find the diffusion coefficients of electrons and holes (μ_p = 480 cm²/(V s)) [3].\n\n**Q2. The diode equation (10 marks)** A diode has I_S = 10^-14 A and n = 1. (a) Find the current at 0.60 V, 0.65 V and 0.70 V [3]. (b) Find the voltage for I = 1 mA [2]. (c) By what voltage must V_D increase to raise the current by a factor of 10, and of 2? [2] (d) Find the small-signal resistance at 1 mA [1]. (e) The forward voltage at a fixed current falls about 2 mV per °C: find its change from 25 °C to 75 °C [2].\n\n**Q3. Diode circuits (14 marks)** (a) A 5 V source drives a 1 kΩ resistor in series with a silicon diode. Find the diode voltage and current using the exponential model and compare with the 0.7 V model [4]. (b) Two silicon diodes in series with 10 V and 2.2 kΩ: find the current [2]. (c) A diode OR gate has inputs of 5 V and 3 V, each through a diode to a common output with a 10 kΩ load to ground: find the output and the state of each diode [4]. (d) A 10 V peak sine wave drives an output node through a series resistor; from the output node a diode (anode at the node) in series with a 3 V battery (positive terminal at the diode's cathode) returns to ground. Describe the output waveform and give its peak [4].\n\n**Q4. Rectifiers (14 marks)** A transformer delivers 12 V rms at 50 Hz. (a) Find the peak output of a full-wave bridge rectifier (0.7 V per diode) and the unfiltered average [3]. (b) With a 500 Ω load find the capacitor needed for a ripple of 1 percent of the peak [3]. (c) For a half-wave rectifier with a 2200 μF capacitor and a 200 Ω load find the peak, ripple and average output, and the PIV required [5]. (d) Compare the half-wave and full-wave circuits [3].\n\n**Q5. Zener regulator (10 marks)** A 12 V supply feeds a 5.1 V Zener (in reverse breakdown) through R_s = 220 Ω; the load is 470 Ω. (a) Find the currents in R_s, the load and the Zener, and the Zener power [4]. (b) What happens if the load is 100 Ω? [3] (c) State the design rule for choosing R_s [3].\n\n**Q6. LEDs and applications (12 marks)** (a) Choose the series resistor for a red LED (V_F = 2.0 V, 15 mA) from 5 V and find its power [3]. (b) Three LEDs (2.0 V each) in series at 20 mA from 12 V: find the resistor [3]. (c) A 3.3 V microcontroller drives a 1.8 V LED at 10 mA: find the resistor [2]. (d) Explain how a diode clamper and a diode clipper differ and give one application of each [4].\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 1: mass action: p = n_i²/N_D = (1.5 × 10^10)²/(5 × 10^15) = 2.25 × 10^20/(5 × 10^15) = **4.5 × 10^4 cm^-3** (about 10^-11 of the electron concentration).\nStep 2: since n ≈ N_D = 5 × 10^15 cm^-3, the resistivity is ρ = 1/(q μ_n n) = 1/(1.602 × 10^-19 × 1350 × 5 × 10^15) = 1/1.081 = **0.925 Ω cm**.\n(b) Step 1: N_A N_D = 10^17 × 10^16 = 10^33 cm^-6 (values from the question) and n_i² = 2.25 × 10^20.\nStep 2: V_0 = V_T ln(N_A N_D/n_i²) = 0.02585 × ln(10^33/(2.25 × 10^20)) = 0.02585 × ln(4.44 × 10^12) = 0.02585 × 29.12 = **0.753 V**.\n(c) Einstein relation D = μ V_T: D_n = 1350 × 0.02585 = **34.9 cm²/s**; D_p = 480 × 0.02585 = **12.4 cm²/s**.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) I = I_S e^(V/V_T) with I_S = 10^-14 A and V_T = 0.02585 V:\n0.60 V: V/V_T = 23.21, e^23.21 = 1.20 × 10^10, so I = 10^-14 × 1.20 × 10^10 = **0.120 mA**.\n0.65 V: V/V_T = 25.15, e^25.15 = 8.32 × 10^10, so I = **0.832 mA**.\n0.70 V: V/V_T = 27.08, e^27.08 = 5.76 × 10^11, so I = **5.76 mA**.\n(b) V = V_T ln(I/I_S) = 0.02585 × ln(10^-3/10^-14) = 0.02585 × ln(10^11) = 0.02585 × 25.33 = **0.655 V**.\n(c) Step 1: per decade of current ΔV = V_T ln 10 = 0.02585 × 2.303 = **59.5 mV**.\nStep 2: for a factor of 2: V_T ln 2 = 0.02585 × 0.693 = **17.9 mV**.\n(d) r_d = V_T/I = 25.85 mV/1 mA = **25.9 Ω**.\n(e) ΔV = -2 mV/°C × 50 °C = **-100 mV**: a 0.70 V drop becomes about 0.60 V at 75 °C. (This property is used in temperature sensors; it also warns against parallel diodes or BJTs sharing current without matching or emitter resistors: thermal runaway.)\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Step 1: KVL: 5 - V_D = I R with I = I_S (e^(V_D/V_T) - 1) = 10^-14 (e^(V_D/0.02585) - 1) and R = 1 kΩ.\nStep 2: solve numerically (iterate): start with V_D = 0.7: I = 4.3 mA; the diode equation gives V_D = V_T ln(I/I_S) = 0.02585 × ln(4.3 × 10^-3/10^-14) = 0.6925 V; repeating gives **V_D = 0.6925 V, I = (5 - 0.6925)/1000 = 4.31 mA**.\nStep 3: the 0.7 V model gives I = (5 - 0.7)/1000 = 4.30 mA: the simple model is accurate to 0.2 percent for this circuit, because the exponential makes V_D insensitive to the current.\n(b) I = (10 - 2 × 0.7)/2.2 kΩ = 8.6/2200 = **3.91 mA**.\n(c) The diode from the 5 V input conducts (assume the diode on): V_out = 5 - 0.7 = **4.3 V**, I = 0.43 mA. The 3 V diode is **reverse-biased** (anode 3 V, cathode 4.3 V): off. Check: the 5 V diode current is positive and the other has a reverse voltage of 1.3 V: consistent. The gate outputs the larger input minus 0.7 V (a logic OR with a 0.7 V loss).\n(d) The diode conducts only when the output node exceeds 3 + 0.7 = 3.7 V. Below that it is off and the output follows the input (no current in the series resistor). When the input rises above 3.7 V the diode conducts and holds the output at **3.7 V** (the excess voltage drops across the series resistor). The negative half-cycle passes unchanged down to -10 V. This is a **positive clipper (limiter)**: output peak **+3.7 V**, negative peak -10 V.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Step 1: V_p = 12 sqrt 2 = 16.97 V.\nStep 2: bridge (two diode drops): **peak = 16.97 - 1.4 = 15.57 V**.\nStep 3: unfiltered average = 2 V_peak/π = 2 × 15.57/π = **9.91 V**.\n(b) Step 1: full-wave ripple V_r = V_p/(2 f R C); for V_r = 0.01 × 15.57 = 0.1557 V.\nStep 2: C = V_p/(2 f R V_r) = 15.57/(2 × 50 × 500 × 0.1557) = 15.57/7785 = **2.0 × 10^-3 F = 2000 μF** (values f = 50 Hz and R = 500 Ω from the question).\n(c) Step 1: half-wave: peak = 16.97 - 0.7 = **16.27 V**.\nStep 2: ripple V_r = V_p/(f R C) = 16.27/(50 × 200 × 2200 × 10^-6) = 16.27/22 = **0.74 V**.\nStep 3: average ≈ 16.27 - 0.74/2 = **15.9 V**.\nStep 4: **PIV**: with a capacitor the reverse voltage across the diode at the negative peak is V_s,peak + V_C ≈ 16.97 + 16.27 = **33.2 V** (about 2 V_p), so use a diode rated for at least 50 V.\n(d) The full-wave bridge delivers twice the ripple frequency (100 Hz), so for the same ripple it needs **half the capacitance**; its output is larger on average; it uses the transformer in both half-cycles (better utilisation) but loses 1.4 V in two diodes; half-wave is cheaper (one diode) but has twice the ripple and may saturate the transformer with dc.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Step 1: I_Rs = (12 - 5.1)/220 = **31.4 mA**.\nStep 2: I_L = 5.1/470 = **10.9 mA**.\nStep 3: I_Z = I_Rs - I_L = 31.4 - 10.9 = **20.5 mA**.\nStep 4: P_Z = 5.1 × 20.5 mA = **105 mW** (a 0.5 W Zener is adequate).\n(b) Step 1: for a 100 Ω load the load would need 5.1/100 = 51 mA, but the series resistor can supply at most 31.4 mA (with the Zener off).\nStep 2: the output falls to 12 × 100/(220 + 100) = **3.75 V**, below the Zener voltage: the Zener stops conducting and **regulation is lost**.\n(c) Choose R_s so that I_Z stays above the minimum knee current (a few mA) at the **maximum load current and minimum input voltage**, and so that the Zener power rating is not exceeded at **no load and maximum input voltage**: R_s ≤ (V_in,min - V_Z)/(I_L,max + I_Z,min) and R_s ≥ (V_in,max - V_Z)/(I_Z,max). For higher efficiency and heavier loads use a linear regulator IC or a switching regulator.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Step 1: R = (5 - 2)/15 mA = **200 Ω**.\nStep 2: P = I² R = (0.015)² × 200 = **45 mW** (a 1/4 W resistor is ample).\n(b) Step 1: R = (12 - 3 × 2)/20 mA = 6/0.02 = **300 Ω** (three LEDs of 2 V in series).\nStep 2: P = 0.02² × 300 = 0.12 W.\n(c) R = (3.3 - 1.8)/10 mA = **150 Ω**.\n(d) A **clipper** removes the part of a waveform above (or below) a reference level (the signal is limited): used for input protection (diodes to the supply rails) and waveform shaping. A **clamper** adds a dc level to an ac signal with a capacitor and a diode (the waveform keeps its shape but its peaks are shifted to a reference level): used for dc restoration in video and level shifting.\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 2: Bipolar Junction Transistors (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. BJT fundamentals (8 marks)** A silicon npn BJT has β = 100 and operates in the active region. (a) For I_B = 40 μA find I_C, I_E and α [3]. (b) By how much must V_BE increase to double I_C? [2] (c) State the bias conditions of the four modes of operation (active, saturation, cutoff, reverse-active) [3].\n\n**Q2. Fixed bias (8 marks)** V_CC = 12 V, R_B = 282 kΩ, R_C = 2 kΩ, V_BE = 0.7 V. (a) Find I_B, I_C and V_CE for β = 100 [3]. (b) Repeat for β = 50 and β = 200 and comment on the sensitivity [5].\n\n**Q3. Voltage-divider bias (14 marks)** V_CC = 12 V, R_1 = 47 kΩ, R_2 = 10 kΩ, R_C = 4.7 kΩ, R_E = 1 kΩ, β = 100. (a) Find the Thevenin equivalent of the base network [3]. (b) Find I_B, I_C and V_CE [5]. (c) Repeat for β = 50 and 200 and compare with Q2 [4]. (d) State the design rule that makes the circuit insensitive to β [2].\n\n**Q4. Load line and Q-point (8 marks)** For the circuit of Q2 with R_C = 2 kΩ and V_CC = 12 V: (a) draw (give the endpoints of) the DC load line [2]. (b) Locate the Q-point for I_C = 4 mA and find the maximum symmetric collector-voltage swing before clipping (assume V_CE(sat) = 0.2 V) [4]. (c) Find the value of β at which the transistor saturates in the fixed bias circuit [2].\n\n**Q5. BJT as a switch (8 marks)** A 5 V logic signal switches a 20 mA load from a 12 V supply using an npn transistor with a forced β of 10. (a) Find the base current and the base resistor [4]. (b) Find the power dissipated in the transistor when on (V_CE(sat) = 0.2 V) and when off [2]. (c) Why is a protection diode used across an inductive load? [2]\n\n**Q6. Small-signal amplifier (16 marks)** For the voltage-divider circuit of Q3 (β = 100, I_C = 1.29 mA, V_A = 100 V), with the emitter bypassed: (a) find g_m, r_π and r_o [3]. (b) Draw the small-signal model and find A_v for R_L = 10 kΩ (including r_o) [4]. (c) Find R_in and R_out [3]. (d) Repeat A_v approximately when the emitter resistor is not bypassed (R_E = 1 kΩ) [3]. (e) Find the voltage gain and input resistance of an emitter follower with R_E = 1 kΩ and R_L = 1 kΩ [3].\n\n**Q7. Gain in decibels and cascading (8 marks)** (a) Express a gain of -159 in dB [2]. (b) A two-stage amplifier has stage gains of -159 and +0.96. Find the overall gain in V/V and dB [3]. (c) A stage with R_out = 4.4 kΩ drives a 10 kΩ load: find the gain loss from loading [3].\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 1: I_C = β I_B = 100 × 40 μA = **4.0 mA**.\nStep 2: I_E = (β + 1) I_B = 101 × 40 μA = **4.04 mA**.\nStep 3: α = β/(β + 1) = 100/101 = **0.990**.\n(b) I_C ∝ e^(V_BE/V_T), so doubling requires ΔV_BE = V_T ln 2 = **17.9 mV**.\n(c) **Active:** BE forward, BC reverse (amplifier). **Saturation:** both forward (switch on, V_CE ≈ 0.2 V). **Cutoff:** both reverse (switch off). **Reverse-active:** BE reverse, BC forward (poor gain, rarely used).\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Step 1: I_B = (V_CC - V_BE)/R_B = (12 - 0.7)/282 kΩ = **40.1 μA**.\nStep 2: I_C = β I_B = 100 × 40.1 μA = **4.0 mA**.\nStep 3: V_CE = V_CC - I_C R_C = 12 - 4.0 mA × 2 kΩ = **4.0 V** (active).\n(b) Step 1: β = 50: I_C = 50 × 40.1 μA = 2.0 mA, V_CE = 12 - 2.0 × 2 = **8.0 V**.\nStep 2: β = 200: I_C would be 200 × 40.1 μA = 8.0 mA and V_CE = 12 - 16 = -4 V: impossible, so the transistor **saturates** (V_CE ≈ 0.2 V, I_C ≈ (12 - 0.2)/2 kΩ = 5.9 mA).\nThe collector current and Q-point change by a factor of four over the β range: **fixed bias is unusable for linear amplifiers** (it is acceptable for switches with a large forced overdrive).\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Step 1: V_th = V_CC R_2/(R_1 + R_2) = 12 × 10/(47 + 10) = **2.105 V**.\nStep 2: R_th = R_1 ∥ R_2 = 47 × 10/57 = **8.25 kΩ**.\n(b) Step 1: KVL on the base loop: V_th = I_B R_th + V_BE + (β + 1) I_B R_E.\nStep 2: I_B = (2.105 - 0.7)/(8.25 kΩ + 101 × 1 kΩ) = 1.405/109.25 kΩ = **12.9 μA**.\nStep 3: **I_C = β I_B = 1.29 mA**; I_E = 1.30 mA.\nStep 4: V_CE = V_CC - I_C R_C - I_E R_E = 12 - 6.05 - 1.30 = **4.66 V** (active, near the centre of the supply).\n(c) β = 50: I_B = 1.405/(8.25 + 51) kΩ = 23.7 μA, **I_C = 1.19 mA**, V_CE = 5.22 V. β = 200: I_B = 1.405/(8.25 + 201) kΩ = 6.7 μA, **I_C = 1.34 mA**, V_CE = 4.34 V. A fourfold change in β changes I_C by only 13 percent (±6 percent), compared with a fourfold change of I_C in the fixed-bias circuit.\n(d) Make **R_th ≪ (β + 1) R_E** (about R_th ≤ 0.1(β + 1) R_E, here 8.25 kΩ against 101 kΩ), so that I_C ≈ (V_th - V_BE)/R_E = 1.4 mA, independent of β; the emitter resistor provides negative (DC) feedback.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) The load line joins (V_CE = 12 V, I_C = 0) and (V_CE = 0, I_C = V_CC/R_C = 12/2 kΩ = 6 mA).\n(b) Step 1: At I_C = 4 mA: V_CE = 12 - 8 = 4 V.\nStep 2: The collector voltage can swing down to V_CE(sat) = 0.2 V (a decrease of 3.8 V) and up to V_CC = 12 V (an increase of 8 V towards cutoff): the symmetric swing is limited by the smaller side: **±3.8 V** (signal peak of 3.8 V).\nStep 3: Centring the Q-point at V_CE = 6.1 V (I_C = 2.95 mA) would give ±5.9 V.\n(c) Step 1: saturation begins when I_C = (12 - 0.2)/2 kΩ = 5.9 mA.\nStep 2: with I_B = 40.1 μA the required β = I_C/I_B = 5.9 mA/40.1 μA = **147** (above this the transistor saturates).\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Step 1: forced β = 10: I_B = I_C/10 = 20 mA/10 = **2 mA**.\nStep 2: R_B = (5 - 0.7)/2 mA = **2.15 kΩ** (use 2.2 kΩ).\n(b) On: P = V_CE(sat) I_C + V_BE I_B = 0.2 × 20 mA + 0.7 × 2 mA = 4 + 1.4 = **5.4 mW**. Off: I_C ≈ 0, so P ≈ **0**. (A switch dissipates little in either state: the heating occurs during the transitions.)\n(c) When the transistor turns off, the inductor current cannot change instantly: L di/dt creates a large voltage spike (hundreds of volts) that would destroy the transistor. A **flyback (freewheeling) diode** across the coil provides a path for the current and clamps the voltage to about 0.7 V above the supply.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Step 1: g_m = I_C/V_T = 1.29 mA/25.85 mV = **49.8 mS**.\nStep 2: r_π = β/g_m = 100/49.8 mS = **2.01 kΩ**.\nStep 3: r_o = V_A/I_C = 100/1.29 mA = **77.7 kΩ**.\n(b) The small-signal model: a source v_be controlling a current g_m v_be from collector to emitter, with r_π between base and emitter, r_o between collector and emitter. With the coupling capacitors as shorts, the output sees R_C ∥ R_L ∥ r_o = 4.7 k ∥ 10 k ∥ 77.7 k = 3.07 kΩ: **A_v = -g_m (R_C ∥ R_L ∥ r_o) = -49.8 mS × 3.07 kΩ = -153** (without r_o: -159).\n(c) **R_in** = R_1 ∥ R_2 ∥ r_π = 8.25 k ∥ 2.01 k = **1.62 kΩ**; **R_out** = R_C ∥ r_o = **4.43 kΩ**.\n(d) Unbypassed R_E: **A_v ≈ -R_C ∥ R_L/R_E = -3.2** (more precisely -g_m R_C'/(1 + g_m R_E) = -153/(1 + 49.8) ≈ -3.0): much smaller, but stable and almost independent of β and temperature; R_in rises to R_th ∥ [r_π + (β + 1) R_E] = 8.25 k ∥ 103 k = 7.6 kΩ.\n(e) Step 1: r_e = 1/g_m = 20.1 Ω; R_L' = R_E ∥ R_L = 500 Ω.\nStep 2: **A_v = R_L'/(R_L' + r_e) = 500/520 = 0.961**.\nStep 3: input resistance at the base: **R_in(base) = (β + 1)(r_e + R_L') = 101 × 520 = 52.5 kΩ**; the output resistance is small (about r_e + R_s/(β + 1)). It is a buffer: nearly unity gain, high input and low output resistance.\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) 20 log10(159) = 20 × 2.2014 = **44.0 dB** (and the phase is inverted: -159 or 44 dB with a 180° shift).\n(b) Step 1: overall gain = (-159)(0.96) = **-153 V/V**.\nStep 2: in dB: 44.0 + 20 log10(0.96) = 44.0 - 0.35 = **43.7 dB**.\n(c) Step 1: the load divides the open-circuit output: R_L/(R_out + R_L) = 10/(4.4 + 10) = **0.69**.\nStep 2: loss = 20 log10(0.69) = -3.2 dB. This is why a buffer (emitter follower or source follower) is placed between a high-output-resistance stage and a low-resistance load.\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 3: MOSFETs, CMOS, Amplifiers and Frequency Response (80 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. MOSFET regions (12 marks)** An NMOS transistor has V_t = 1 V and k_n = 2 mA/V² (I_D = (1/2) k_n V_ov² in saturation). (a) Find the region and drain current for V_GS = 0.5 V; for V_GS = 3 V and V_DS = 0.5 V; for V_GS = 3 V and V_DS = 5 V [6]. (b) Find the on-resistance for small V_DS at V_GS = 5 V [2]. (c) Explain the pinch-off condition and why the current saturates [2]. (d) How does a PMOS device differ? [2]\n\n**Q2. DC bias (12 marks)** (a) V_DD = 10 V, V_GS = 3 V, R_D = 1 kΩ: find I_D, V_DS and verify saturation [4]. (b) A voltage-divider circuit has R_1 = 2 MΩ, R_2 = 1 MΩ, R_S = 1 kΩ, R_D = 3 kΩ, V_DD = 10 V: find V_G, I_D, V_S and V_DS [6]. (c) Why does the source resistor stabilise the bias? [2]\n\n**Q3. Common-source amplifier (14 marks)** The transistor of Q2(a) has λ = 0.02 V^-1. (a) Find g_m and r_o at I_D = 4 mA [3]. (b) Find the gain for R_D = 1 kΩ with R_L = ∞ and with R_L = 1 kΩ (including r_o) [5]. (c) State R_in and R_out [2]. (d) Find the gain of the source follower with R_S = 1 kΩ and R_L = ∞ [2]. (e) Compare CS and CE amplifiers [2].\n\n**Q4. CMOS inverter (12 marks)** V_DD = 3.3 V, V_tn = 0.6 V, V_tp = -0.6 V. (a) Explain the operation for V_in = 0 and V_in = V_DD and why static power is near zero [3]. (b) Find the switching threshold V_M for k_n = k_p, and for k_p = k_n/4 [4]. (c) A gate with 20 fF load switches at 1 GHz with activity 0.1: find the dynamic power [2]. (d) Estimate the propagation delay for R_on = 10 kΩ [1]. (e) Draw the transistor-level structure of a 2-input NAND [2].\n\n**Q5. Frequency response (10 marks)** (a) A CE amplifier has an input coupling capacitor of 1 μF seeing 11 kΩ: find the low-frequency cut-off [2]. (b) A stage with gain -10, C_gd = 2 pF and C_gs = 10 pF is driven from 10 kΩ: find the Miller input capacitance and the upper cut-off [4]. (c) An op-amp has a gain-bandwidth product of 1 MHz: find the bandwidth for a closed-loop gain of 100 [2]. (d) Define the unity-gain frequency [2].\n\n**Q6. Differential pair and op-amps (10 marks)** (a) A MOS differential pair has g_m = 4 mS and R_D = 5 kΩ: find the differential gain [2]. (b) If the common-mode gain is 0.01, find the CMRR in dB [3]. (c) Name four non-ideal properties of a real op-amp and the effect of each [4]. (d) What does the tail current source do? [1]\n\n**Q7. Power and thermal design (6 marks)** A transistor dissipates 2 W with θ_JA = 50 °C/W in a 25 °C ambient. (a) Find the junction temperature [2]. (b) What heat-sink thermal resistance is needed to keep T_J ≤ 100 °C at 5 W with θ_JC = 5 °C/W and θ_CS = 1 °C/W, ambient 40 °C? [4]\n\n**Q8. Design choice (4 marks)** For (i) a high-gain low-noise audio preamplifier, (ii) a microprocessor and (iii) a relay driver, choose BJT or MOSFET and justify.\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Case 1: V_GS = 0.5 V < V_t: **cutoff**, I_D = 0.\nCase 2: V_GS = 3 V: V_ov = 3 - 1 = 2 V; V_DS = 0.5 V < 2 V: **triode**: I_D = k_n [V_ov V_DS - V_DS²/2] = 2 mA × (2 × 0.5 - 0.125) = 2 mA × 0.875 = **1.75 mA**.\nCase 3: V_GS = 3 V and V_DS = 5 V ≥ 2 V: **saturation**: I_D = 0.5 × 2 mA × 2² = **4.0 mA**.\n(b) At V_GS = 5 V: V_ov = 4 V; r_on = 1/(k_n V_ov) = 1/(2 mA/V² × 4 V) = **125 Ω**.\n(c) When V_DS reaches V_ov the channel depth at the drain end falls to zero (**pinch-off**); further increase of V_DS drops across the pinched-off region and does not increase the current significantly (the current is set by the charge in the rest of the channel, controlled by V_GS). The small remaining slope is channel-length modulation (λ).\n(d) A PMOS device has holes as carriers, V_t negative, and V_GS, V_DS, I_D reversed (current flows from source to drain; the source is connected to the supply). For the same size its current is about 2 to 3 times lower, so PMOS transistors are made wider in CMOS.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Step 1: I_D = 0.5 k_n (V_GS - V_t)² = 0.5 × 2 mA × (3 - 1)² = **4 mA**.\nStep 2: V_DS = V_DD - I_D R_D = 10 - 4 mA × 1 kΩ = **6 V**.\nStep 3: V_ov = 2 V ≤ V_DS = 6 V: **saturation confirmed**.\n(b) Step 1: V_G = 10 × 1/(2 + 1) = **3.33 V**.\nStep 2: I_D = (1/2)(2 mA)(V_G - I_D R_S - V_t)² = 1 mA × (2.333 - I_D × 1 kΩ)²; with I_D in mA: I = (2.333 - I)², so I² - 5.667 I + 5.444 = 0.\nStep 3: I = (5.667 ± sqrt(32.11 - 21.78))/2 = (5.667 ± 3.214)/2, giving **I_D = 1.23 mA** (the other root, 4.44 mA, would give V_GS below V_t).\nStep 4: V_S = 1.23 V, V_GS = 3.33 - 1.23 = 2.11 V, V_ov = 1.11 V. V_D = 10 - 1.23 × 3 = 6.31 V, **V_DS = 6.31 - 1.23 = 5.08 V ≥ V_ov**: saturation confirmed.\n(c) If k_n or V_t increases, the current tends to rise; the larger drop I_D R_S reduces V_GS and counteracts the rise (negative DC feedback), so the bias current varies much less than in the circuit without R_S.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Step 1: g_m = k_n V_ov = 2 mA/V² × 2 V = **4 mS** (= 2 I_D/V_ov = 8 mA/2 V ✓).\nStep 2: r_o = 1/(λ I_D) = 1/(0.02 × 4 mA) = **12.5 kΩ**.\n(b) Step 1: R_L = ∞: R_D ∥ r_o = 1 k ∥ 12.5 k = 0.926 kΩ; A_v = -g_m (R_D ∥ r_o) = -4 mS × 0.926 kΩ = **-3.70**.\nStep 2: R_L = 1 kΩ: R_D ∥ R_L ∥ r_o = 1 ∥ 1 ∥ 12.5 = 0.481 kΩ: **A_v = -4 mS × 0.481 kΩ = -1.92**.\n(c) **R_in = R_G** (the gate draws no current: megohms, set by the bias resistors, here 2 M ∥ 1 M = 667 kΩ); **R_out = R_D ∥ r_o = 926 Ω**.\n(d) A_v = g_m R_S/(1 + g_m R_S) = 4/(1 + 4) = **0.80**; R_out ≈ 1/g_m = 250 Ω.\n(e) Both stages invert the signal. For the same bias current a BJT has a much higher g_m (g_m = I_C/V_T = 38.7 mS per mA) than a MOSFET (g_m = 2 I_D/V_ov = 10 mS per mA at V_ov = 0.2 V, 1 mS per mA at V_ov = 2 V), so the CE stage gives more gain. The CS stage has an essentially infinite input resistance at low frequencies (it loads the source far less), while the CE input resistance is r_π (a few kΩ).\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) **V_in = 0:** the NMOS is off (V_GS = 0 < V_t) and the PMOS is on (V_SG = V_DD > |V_t|): the output is pulled up to V_DD. **V_in = V_DD:** the NMOS is on and the PMOS off: the output is pulled to 0. In each steady state one transistor is off, so there is **no path from V_DD to ground**: the static current is only leakage, and power is consumed essentially only when the output switches (charging and discharging C_L).\n(b) V_M = (V_tn + r (V_DD - |V_tp|))/(1 + r) with r = sqrt(k_p/k_n).\nStep 1: equal k: r = 1: V_M = (0.6 + 2.7)/2 = **1.65 V** (V_DD/2).\nStep 2: k_p = k_n/4: r = 0.5: V_M = (0.6 + 0.5 × 2.7)/1.5 = 1.95/1.5 = **1.30 V** (the weaker PMOS lowers the threshold).\n(c) P = α C V_DD² f = 0.1 × 20 × 10^-15 × 3.3² × 10^9 = 0.1 × 20 × 10^-15 × 10.89 × 10^9 = **21.8 μW**.\n(d) t_p ≈ 0.69 R_on C_L = 0.69 × 10 kΩ × 20 fF = **0.14 ns**.\n(e) NAND2: two NMOS in **series** between the output and ground; two PMOS in **parallel** between V_DD and the output; each input drives one NMOS and one PMOS. The output is low only when both inputs are high.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) f_L = 1/(2π R C) = 1/(2π × 11 kΩ × 1 μF) = 1/(0.0691) = **14.5 Hz**.\n(b) Step 1: Miller: C_in = C_gs + C_gd (1 - A_v) = 10 + 2 × (1 + 10) = **32 pF**.\nStep 2: f_H = 1/(2π R_s C_in) = 1/(2π × 10 kΩ × 32 pF) = **497 kHz**.\n(c) GBW = A × BW: BW = 1 MHz/100 = **10 kHz**.\n(d) The **unity-gain frequency** f_T is where the open-loop gain magnitude falls to 1 (0 dB); for a single-pole op-amp it equals the gain-bandwidth product; it measures the speed of the device.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) A_d = g_m R_D = 4 mS × 5 kΩ = **20** (26 dB).\n(b) CMRR = |A_d/A_cm| = 20/0.01 = 2000; in dB: 20 log10(2000) = **66 dB**.\n(c) Examples: **finite gain and bandwidth** (the gain falls with frequency: GBW limits the closed-loop bandwidth); **input offset voltage and bias currents** (a dc error at the output, proportional to the gain); **limited slew rate** (the output cannot change faster than a V/μs limit, distorting large fast signals); **limited output swing and drive current** (clipping near the rails, low-impedance loads); finite input resistance and noise. (Any four with effects.)\n(d) The tail current source fixes the sum of the two branch currents (a high-impedance source rejects common-mode signals and sets the bias of the pair).\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) T_J = T_A + P θ_JA = 25 + 2 × 50 = **125 °C** (near the 150 °C maximum; a heat sink or lower power is advisable).\n(b) Step 1: T_J - T_A = P (θ_JC + θ_CS + θ_SA): 100 - 40 = 60 °C = 5 W × (5 + 1 + θ_SA).\nStep 2: θ_JC + θ_CS + θ_SA = 60/5 = 12 °C/W, so θ_SA = 12 - 6 = 6 °C/W: **θ_SA ≤ 6 °C/W**.\n\n**Q8.**\n(i) A **BJT** (or a JFET) front end: higher g_m per current and lower noise for low-frequency, low-impedance sources; (ii) **MOSFETs (CMOS)**: negligible static power, high density, easy integration of billions of switches; (iii) a **BJT or a power MOSFET**: a BJT with a base resistor and a flyback diode is cheap and simple for small relays, a logic-level MOSFET gives a lower on-resistance and no base current for larger loads. (1 mark each for choice and reason; 1 mark for the relay diode.)\n","exam":"# Electronics I (Semiconductor Devices): Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks. Use V_T = 25 mV, V_BE = 0.7 V, a 0.7 V silicon diode drop and n_i = 1.5 × 10^10 cm^-3 unless stated.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Doping silicon with phosphorus produces\nA) p-type material  B) n-type material  C) an insulator  D) intrinsic material\n\n**2.** The reverse saturation current of a diode roughly doubles every\nA) 1 °C  B) 5 to 10 °C  C) 50 °C  D) never\n\n**3.** The depletion region of a pn junction under forward bias\nA) widens  B) narrows  C) disappears and the diode breaks down  D) is unchanged\n\n**4.** For a bridge rectifier from a transformer with peak secondary voltage 20 V and silicon diodes, the peak output is\nA) 20 V  B) 19.3 V  C) 18.6 V  D) 10 V\n\n**5.** In a capacitor-filtered full-wave rectifier the ripple frequency (50 Hz mains) is\nA) 25 Hz  B) 50 Hz  C) 100 Hz  D) 200 Hz\n\n**6.** A Zener diode regulates the output voltage because in breakdown\nA) the current is constant  B) the voltage stays nearly constant while the current changes  C) it is an open circuit  D) it conducts only for forward bias\n\n**7.** The current gain β of a BJT is the ratio\nA) I_E/I_B  B) I_C/I_B  C) I_C/I_E  D) I_B/I_C\n\n**8.** A BJT saturates when\nA) both junctions are forward-biased  B) both are reverse-biased  C) only BE is reverse-biased  D) V_CE is very large\n\n**9.** A BJT bias circuit is insensitive to β if\nA) R_th ≪ (β + 1) R_E  B) R_th ≫ (β + 1) R_E  C) R_E = 0  D) R_C = 0\n\n**10.** The small-signal input resistance r_π of a BJT equals\nA) V_T/I_C  B) β/g_m  C) V_A/I_C  D) β V_T\n\n**11.** The Early voltage V_A describes\nA) the supply voltage  B) the finite output resistance r_o = V_A/I_C  C) the base current  D) the breakdown voltage\n\n**12.** The input resistance of a common-collector stage is\nA) very low  B) high  C) zero  D) equal to R_C\n\n**13.** An NMOS transistor with V_GS = 3 V, V_t = 1 V and V_DS = 0.5 V is in the\nA) cutoff region  B) triode region  C) saturation region  D) breakdown region\n\n**14.** The saturation drain current of a MOSFET is proportional to\nA) V_ov  B) V_ov²  C) 1/V_ov  D) V_DS\n\n**15.** The transconductance of a MOSFET in saturation is g_m =\nA) I_D/V_ov  B) 2 I_D/V_ov  C) I_D V_ov  D) V_ov/I_D\n\n**16.** In a CMOS NAND gate the NMOS transistors are\nA) in parallel  B) in series  C) absent  D) replaced by resistors\n\n**17.** Dynamic power in CMOS is proportional to\nA) V_DD  B) V_DD²  C) 1/V_DD  D) independent of V_DD\n\n**18.** The bandwidth of an op-amp circuit with a gain-bandwidth product of 1 MHz and a closed-loop gain of 10 is\nA) 10 kHz  B) 100 kHz  C) 1 MHz  D) 10 MHz\n\n**19.** A current mirror copies a reference current using\nA) two transistors with the same V_GS  B) a resistor only  C) a capacitor  D) a diode only\n\n**20.** The junction temperature of a device dissipating P with thermal resistance θ_JA is\nA) T_A + P/θ_JA  B) T_A + P θ_JA  C) T_A - P θ_JA  D) P θ_JA only\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** n-type silicon has N_D = 2 × 10^16 cm^-3. Find the electron and hole concentrations at 300 K. Then, for p-type silicon with N_A = 2 × 10^16 cm^-3, find the hole and electron concentrations.\n\n**B2. (5)** A diode with a 0.7 V drop is in series with a 1.8 kΩ resistor and a 9 V supply. Find the current, the power in the resistor, and the current if a second identical diode is added in series.\n\n**B3. (5)** Design the series resistor (nearest standard value from 220, 240, 270 Ω) for a Zener regulator: V_in = 15 V, V_Z = 9.1 V, I_L,max = 20 mA, I_Z,min = 5 mA. Check the Zener current at full load and at no load.\n\n**B4. (5)** A BJT (β = 150) has I_B = 20 μA, V_CC = 10 V, R_C = 2 kΩ. Find I_C, V_CE, g_m and r_π.\n\n**B5. (5)** A BJT at I_C = 0.5 mA has β = 100 and V_A = 80 V. Find g_m, r_π and r_o.\n\n**B6. (5)** An NMOS with V_t = 0.7 V and k_n = 1 mA/V² has V_GS = 2.7 V and V_DS = 4 V. Find I_D and g_m, and check the region.\n\n**B7. (5)** A chip has 10^7 gates switching with activity 0.2, load 5 fF each, V_DD = 1.2 V and f = 1 GHz. Find the dynamic power.\n\n**B8. (5)** (a) Express a voltage gain of 100 in dB. (b) Three identical stages each with a gain of 10 V/V are cascaded (ignore loading): give the total gain in V/V and in dB.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. Power supply design.** A 5 V, 0.5 A supply is built from a 50 Hz transformer with a 9 V rms secondary, a bridge rectifier (0.7 V per diode), a smoothing capacitor and a 7805 linear regulator that needs at least 7 V at its input.\n(a) Find the peak of the rectified voltage [2]. (b) Find the capacitor for a peak-to-peak ripple of 1 V at 0.5 A (ripple = I/(2 f C)) [3]. (c) Find the minimum input voltage to the regulator and confirm that the regulator works [2]. (d) Find the power dissipated in the regulator, using the average input voltage (peak minus half the ripple) [2]. (e) Suggest an efficiency improvement [1].\n\n**C2. BJT amplifier design.** Design a common-emitter stage with V_CC = 15 V, β = 120, I_C = 2 mA, R_E = 1 kΩ and V_CE = 7 V.\n(a) Find I_E, V_E, V_B and R_C [4]. (b) Choose R_th = 0.1(β + 1) R_E and find R_1 and R_2 [3]. (c) Find g_m, r_π and the gain for R_L = 10 kΩ with the emitter bypassed (take R_C = 3 kΩ) [3].\n\n**C3. MOSFET amplifier design.** A common-source stage has V_DD = 12 V, k_n = 0.5 mA/V², V_t = 1 V and I_D = 1 mA.\n(a) Find V_ov, V_GS and g_m [3]. (b) Choose R_S = 1 kΩ and R_D for V_D = 8 V; find V_G and the divider (R_1 = 2 MΩ, R_2 = 1 MΩ) [4]. (c) Find the gain (R_L = ∞) and the output resistance [3].\n\n**C4. CMOS and power.** A processor has 10^8 gates, each with an effective switched capacitance of 2 fF, activity 0.1, V_DD = 0.9 V, f = 3 GHz.\n(a) Find the dynamic power [3]. (b) By how much does the power change if V_DD is lowered to 0.8 V at the same frequency, and why can't voltage be lowered freely? [4] (c) Explain why CMOS draws almost no static current and name one source of static power in modern chips [3].\n\n**C5. Frequency response.** An amplifier has a midband gain of -100 (40 dB), a low-frequency pole at 100 Hz (coupling capacitor) and a high-frequency pole at 1 MHz.\n(a) Find the gain in dB at 10 Hz, 100 Hz, 10 kHz and 10 MHz (single-pole approximations) [4]. (b) Find the bandwidth [2]. (c) Estimate the high-frequency pole when the source resistance is 10 kΩ and the input capacitance is 32 pF (including the Miller effect) [2]. (d) Explain how negative feedback trades gain for bandwidth [2].\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **B** · 2 **B** · 3 **B** · 4 **C** · 5 **C** · 6 **B** · 7 **B** · 8 **A** · 9 **A** · 10 **B** · 11 **B** · 12 **B** · 13 **B** · 14 **B** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **B** · 18 **B** · 19 **A** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons: 4. 20 - 1.4 = 18.6 V. 5. A full-wave rectifier doubles the frequency. 13. V_ov = 2 V and V_DS = 0.5 V < V_ov. 18. 1 MHz/10 = 100 kHz.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** n-type: Step 1: n ≈ N_D = **2 × 10^16 cm^-3**.\nStep 2: p = n_i²/n = 2.25 × 10^20/(2 × 10^16) = **1.125 × 10^4 cm^-3**.\np-type with N_A = 2 × 10^16: Step 3: the majority holes p ≈ **2 × 10^16 cm^-3**. Step 4: the minority electrons n = n_i²/p = **1.125 × 10^4 cm^-3**.\n\n**B2.** Step 1: I = (9 - 0.7)/1.8 kΩ = **4.61 mA**.\nStep 2: P_R = I² R = (4.61 mA)² × 1.8 kΩ = **38 mW**.\nStep 3: with two diodes: I = (9 - 1.4)/1.8 kΩ = **4.22 mA**.\n\n**B3.** Step 1: R_s ≤ (15 - 9.1)/(20 + 5) mA = 5.9/0.025 = 236 Ω, so choose the next lower standard value **220 Ω**.\nStep 2: I_Rs = 5.9/220 = 26.8 mA.\nStep 3: full load: I_Z = 26.8 - 20 = **6.8 mA** (> 5 mA ✓).\nStep 4: no load: I_Z = **26.8 mA** (P_Z = 9.1 × 26.8 mA = 244 mW: use a 0.5 W Zener).\n\n**B4.** Step 1: I_C = β I_B = 150 × 20 μA = **3 mA**.\nStep 2: V_CE = 10 - 3 mA × 2 kΩ = **4 V**.\nStep 3: g_m = I_C/V_T = 3 mA/25 mV = **120 mS**.\nStep 4: r_π = β/g_m = 150/0.12 = **1.25 kΩ**.\n\n**B5.** Step 1: g_m = 0.5 mA/25 mV = **20 mS**.\nStep 2: r_π = β/g_m = 100/0.02 = **5 kΩ**.\nStep 3: r_o = V_A/I_C = 80/0.5 mA = **160 kΩ**.\n\n**B6.** Step 1: V_ov = V_GS - V_t = 2.7 - 0.7 = 2 V; V_DS = 4 V ≥ 2 V: **saturation**.\nStep 2: I_D = (1/2)(1 mA/V²)(2 V)² = **2 mA**.\nStep 3: g_m = k_n V_ov = 1 × 2 = **2 mS**.\n\n**B7.** P = α N C V² f = 0.2 × 10^7 × 5 × 10^-15 × 1.44 × 10^9: 0.2 × 10^7 = 2 × 10^6; × 5 × 10^-15 = 10^-8; × 1.44 = 1.44 × 10^-8; × 10^9 = **14.4 W**.\n\n**B8.** (a) 20 log10(100) = 20 × 2 = **40 dB**.\n(b) Total gain 10 × 10 × 10 = **1000 V/V = 60 dB** (the dB gains add: 3 × 20 dB).\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.**\n(a) Step 1: V_p(secondary) = 9 sqrt 2 = 12.73 V.\nStep 2: the bridge output peak = 12.73 - 1.4 = **11.33 V**.\n(b) C = I/(2 f V_r) = 0.5/(2 × 50 × 1) = 0.005 F = **5000 μF** (full-wave: ripple frequency 100 Hz).\n(c) The minimum input voltage = 11.33 - 1.0 = **10.33 V** > 7 V (the regulator's dropout requirement): the regulator works (with a 3.3 V margin).\n(d) Step 1: average input ≈ 11.33 - 0.5 = 10.83 V.\nStep 2: P = (V_in - V_out) I = (10.83 - 5) × 0.5 = **2.9 W**: the regulator needs a heat sink (T_J = T_A + 2.9 θ_JA).\n(e) Use a **switching (buck) regulator** (efficiency over 85 percent) or a low-dropout regulator with a lower transformer voltage to reduce the wasted power.\n\n**C2.**\n(a) Step 1: I_E = I_C (β + 1)/β = 2 mA × 121/120 = 2.017 mA.\nStep 2: V_E = I_E R_E = 2.017 mA × 1 kΩ = **2.02 V**; V_B = V_E + 0.7 = **2.72 V**.\nStep 3: from V_CC = I_C R_C + V_CE + I_E R_E: R_C = (15 - 7 - 2.02)/2 mA = 5.98/2 mA = **2.99 kΩ ≈ 3 kΩ**.\n(b) Step 1: design rule R_th = 0.1 (β + 1) R_E = 0.1 × 121 × 1 kΩ = **12.1 kΩ**.\nStep 2: R_1 = R_th V_CC/V_B = 12.1 × 15/2.72 = **66.8 kΩ** (68 kΩ); R_2 = R_th V_CC/(V_CC - V_B) = 12.1 × 15/12.28 = **14.8 kΩ** (15 kΩ).\nStep 3: with the standard values 68 kΩ and 15 kΩ: V_th = 15 × 15/83 = 2.71 V, I_B = 15.1 μA, I_C = 1.81 mA and V_CE = 7.74 V: close to the design.\n(c) Step 1: g_m = 2 mA/25 mV = **80 mS** (77 mS at 1.93 mA); r_π = β/g_m = **1.5 kΩ**.\nStep 2: A_v = -g_m (R_C ∥ R_L) = -0.08 × (3 k ∥ 10 k = 2.31 kΩ) = **-185**.\nStep 3: with the standard resistors of part (b), I_C = 1.81 mA and g_m = 72 mS, so the gain is about -167.\n\n**C3.**\n(a) Step 1: V_ov = sqrt(2 I_D/k_n) = sqrt(2 × 1 mA/0.5 mA/V²) = **2 V**.\nStep 2: V_GS = V_t + V_ov = **3 V**.\nStep 3: g_m = 2 I_D/V_ov = 2 mA/2 V = **1 mS**.\n(b) Step 1: V_S = I_D R_S = 1 V, so V_G = V_GS + V_S = **4 V**; the divider R_1 = 2 MΩ, R_2 = 1 MΩ gives 12 × 1/3 = 4 V ✓.\nStep 2: for V_D = 8 V: R_D = (12 - 8)/1 mA = **4 kΩ**.\nStep 3: check V_DS = 8 - 1 = 7 V ≥ V_ov: saturation ✓.\n(c) A_v = -g_m R_D = -1 mS × 4 kΩ = **-4** (with the source bypassed; without bypass A_v ≈ -g_m R_D/(1 + g_m R_S) = -2). R_out ≈ R_D = **4 kΩ** (in parallel with r_o).\n\n**C4.**\n(a) P = α N C V² f = 0.1 × 10^8 × 2 × 10^-15 × 0.81 × 3 × 10^9: 0.1 × 10^8 = 10^7; × 2 × 10^-15 = 2 × 10^-8; × 0.81 = 1.62 × 10^-8; × 3 × 10^9 = **48.6 W**.\n(b) Step 1: P ∝ V²: (0.8/0.9)² = 0.79, so P = 0.79 × 48.6 = **38.4 W** (21 percent less).\nStep 2: the supply cannot be lowered freely because the gate delay increases as V_DD approaches the threshold voltage (the overdrive V_DD - V_t shrinks, so the drive current and the maximum frequency fall), noise margins shrink, and variations and leakage become more important. Designers use dynamic voltage and frequency scaling (DVFS).\n(c) In each steady state one transistor of the complementary pair is off, so there is no direct path from V_DD to ground; current flows only when the output switches. **Subthreshold leakage** (the off transistor still conducts a small current, which grows exponentially as V_t is reduced), gate-oxide tunnelling and junction leakage are sources of static power; in modern nanometre chips they can be 20 to 40 percent of the total.\n\n**C5.**\n(a) |H| = 100/sqrt(1 + (f_L/f)²)/sqrt(1 + (f/f_H)²) with f_L = 100 Hz and f_H = 1 MHz.\nStep 1: at 10 Hz: 100/sqrt(1 + 100) = 9.95: **20.0 dB**.\nStep 2: at 100 Hz: 100/sqrt 2 = 70.7: **37.0 dB** (-3 dB).\nStep 3: at 10 kHz: **40.0 dB** (midband).\nStep 4: at 10 MHz: 100/sqrt(1 + 100) = 9.95: **20.0 dB**.\n(b) The bandwidth between the -3 dB points: f_H - f_L = 1 MHz - 100 Hz = **about 1 MHz** (999.9 kHz). The gain-bandwidth product is 100 × 1 MHz = 100 MHz.\n(c) f_H = 1/(2π R_s C_in) = 1/(2π × 10 kΩ × 32 pF) = 1/(2.01 × 10^-6) = **497 kHz**.\n(d) With negative feedback of the feedback factor β_f, the closed-loop gain falls by the factor (1 + A β_f) while all the bandwidth poles move out by the same factor: gain × bandwidth stays constant (A_f = A/(1 + Aβ_f), f_H,f = f_H(1 + Aβ_f)). A gain of 100 reduced to 10 gives a bandwidth of 10 MHz instead of 1 MHz. Feedback also reduces distortion and makes the gain insensitive to the device parameters.\n","notes":"# Electronics I (Semiconductor Devices): Complete Lecture Notes\n\nElectronics I explains how the devices at the heart of every circuit work (diodes, bipolar transistors and MOSFETs) and how to use them in rectifiers, switches and amplifiers. You will combine device physics, circuit analysis (Circuit Analysis I) and approximate models. Constants: thermal voltage V_T = kT/q = 25.85 mV at 300 K (use 25 or 26 mV for quick work), q = 1.602 × 10^-19 C, intrinsic silicon concentration n_i = 1.5 × 10^10 cm^-3 at 300 K. Convention: DC quantities in capital letters with capital subscripts (I_C, V_BE), small-signal quantities in lower case (i_c, v_be), total instantaneous quantities in lower case with capital subscripts (i_C = I_C + i_c).\n\n## Chapter 1. Semiconductor Physics\n\n### 1.1 Intrinsic and doped silicon\nSilicon (group IV) forms a crystal with a **band gap** of 1.12 eV. At 300 K a few electrons are thermally excited into the conduction band, leaving holes in the valence band: **n = p = n_i** in an intrinsic crystal.\n- **Doping:** donors (phosphorus, arsenic: group V) create **n-type** material with majority carriers electrons, n ≈ N_D; acceptors (boron: group III) create **p-type** material with majority carriers holes, p ≈ N_A. The **mass-action law**: n p = n_i² holds in equilibrium, so in n-type silicon with N_D = 10^16 cm^-3, p = n_i²/N_D = 2.25 × 10^4 cm^-3.\n- **Currents:** **drift** (carriers move in an electric field: J = q(μ_n n + μ_p p) E, with mobilities μ_n ≈ 1350 and μ_p ≈ 480 cm²/(V s)) and **diffusion** (carriers move from high to low concentration: J_n = q D_n dn/dx). The Einstein relation D/μ = V_T. Resistivity falls with doping; temperature raises n_i exponentially.\n\n### 1.2 The pn junction\nJoining p and n regions creates a **depletion region** with fixed ionised dopants and a **built-in potential** V_0 = V_T ln(N_A N_D/n_i²). For N_A = 10^17 and N_D = 10^16 cm^-3: V_0 = 0.753 V. The depletion width widens under **reverse bias** and narrows under **forward bias**: w ∝ sqrt(V_0 + V_R).\n- **Forward bias** lowers the barrier, and minority carriers are injected across it: current rises exponentially. **Reverse bias** gives only a tiny saturation current I_S (pA to nA), which doubles about every 5 °C.\n- **Diode equation:** **i = I_S (e^(v/(n V_T)) - 1)**, with the ideality factor n between 1 and 2. For I_S = 10^-14 A: i = 0.83 mA at 0.65 V and 5.76 mA at 0.70 V (a factor of ten per 60 mV). The forward voltage drops about 2 mV per °C (at constant current).\n- **Junction capacitances:** depletion capacitance C_j = C_j0/sqrt(1 + V_R/V_0) (varactors) and diffusion capacitance in forward bias. **Breakdown:** Zener (tunnelling, below about 5 V) and avalanche (above about 7 V); breakdown is non-destructive if the power is limited.\n\n## Chapter 2. Diodes and Diode Circuits\n\n### 2.1 Models\n- **Ideal diode:** a short when forward-biased, an open circuit when reverse-biased.\n- **Constant-voltage-drop model:** forward-conducting diode = voltage source of 0.7 V (silicon) in series with the circuit (0.2 to 0.3 V for germanium or Schottky, about 1.8 to 3 V for LEDs).\n- **Exponential model with graphical or iterative solution:** a 5 V source with 1 kΩ: solving 5 - v = 1000 I_S (e^(v/V_T) - 1) gives V_D = 0.6925 V and I = 4.31 mA (the 0.7 V model gives 4.30 mA: excellent).\n- **Small-signal model:** at a bias current I_D the incremental resistance r_d = n V_T/I_D (6 Ω at 4.3 mA). Use it for ac signals riding on a dc bias.\n- **Analysis method:** guess the state of each diode (on or off), analyse, then check the assumption (current > 0 for diodes assumed on; reverse voltage for diodes assumed off).\n\n### 2.2 Rectifiers and power supplies\n- **Half-wave rectifier:** one diode in series with the load: peak output V_p - 0.7 V, conducting half of each cycle. **Full-wave bridge:** four diodes; two conduct in each half-cycle: peak output V_p - 1.4 V; average (unfiltered) 2 V_peak/π.\n- **Example.** A 12 V rms transformer secondary: V_p = 12 sqrt 2 = 16.97 V; the bridge output peaks at 15.57 V; the unfiltered average is 2(15.57)/π = 9.91 V.\n- **Smoothing capacitor:** the capacitor charges to the peak and discharges through the load: **ripple V_r ≈ V_p/(f R C)** (half-wave) and **V_p/(2 f R C)** (full-wave, f = mains frequency). For f = 50 Hz, R = 500 Ω, C = 1000 μF and V_p = 15.57 V (bridge): V_r = 15.57/(2 × 50 × 500 × 10^-3) = 0.311 V (2 percent ripple). The diodes conduct in short pulses (the conduction angle is small), so the peak diode current is much larger than the average load current. Diode ratings: **PIV** (peak inverse voltage) and surge current.\n- **Voltage regulation:** the Zener regulator: V_in = 12 V through R_s = 220 Ω to a 5.1 V Zener in parallel with a 470 Ω load: I_in = (12 - 5.1)/220 = 31.4 mA; I_L = 5.1/470 = 10.9 mA; I_Z = 20.5 mA; P_Z = 105 mW. Regulation holds if I_Z stays within its limits as V_in and the load change. Modern supplies use linear regulators (LM7805, LDOs) and switching regulators (buck, boost, flyback), which are far more efficient.\n- **Other circuits:** **clippers/limiters** (a diode with a bias voltage limits the output; protection of inputs); **clampers** (a capacitor and a diode shift the dc level of a waveform); **voltage doublers** and charge pumps; **peak detectors**; diode logic (AND/OR); **logarithmic amplifiers**.\n\n### 2.3 Special diodes\n**Schottky** (metal-semiconductor, V_F about 0.3 V and very fast, used in power supplies and RF), **LED** (light by recombination: R = (V_S - V_F)/I_F, for example (5 - 2)/20 mA = 150 Ω), **photodiode** (reverse-biased, current proportional to light), **solar cell**, **Zener**, **varactor**, **TVS** (transient voltage suppressor), **laser diode**.\n\n## Chapter 3. The Bipolar Junction Transistor (BJT)\n\n### 3.1 Structure and operation\nAn **npn** BJT has an emitter (heavily doped), a thin base and a collector. Terminal currents: I_E = I_C + I_B. **Active mode** (forward-active): the base-emitter junction is forward-biased and the base-collector junction is reverse-biased: **I_C = I_S e^(V_BE/V_T)** (the transconductance property), **I_C = β I_B**, I_E = (β + 1) I_B, α = I_C/I_E = β/(β + 1). Typical β = 50 to 300 (it varies widely between devices and with temperature, so good designs do not depend on it). V_BE ≈ 0.7 V.\n- **Cutoff:** both junctions reverse-biased (I_C ≈ 0, the switch is off). **Saturation:** both forward-biased (V_CE(sat) ≈ 0.2 V, I_C < β I_B, the switch is on). **Early effect:** I_C increases slightly with V_CE: I_C = I_S e^(V_BE/V_T)(1 + V_CE/V_A), with output resistance r_o = V_A/I_C. pnp transistors work with all polarities reversed.\n\n### 3.2 DC bias circuits\n- **Fixed bias:** R_B from V_CC to the base: I_B = (V_CC - 0.7)/R_B; I_C = β I_B. With V_CC = 12 V and R_B = 282 kΩ: I_B = 40 μA, I_C = 4.0 mA (β = 100); with R_C = 2 kΩ, V_CE = 12 - 4(2) = 4 V (about the middle of the supply: good for symmetric swing). The Q-point moves in proportion to β: unacceptable.\n- **Voltage-divider (universal) bias with an emitter resistor:** R_1 = 47 kΩ, R_2 = 10 kΩ, V_CC = 12 V: V_th = 12 × 10/57 = 2.105 V, R_th = R_1 ∥ R_2 = 8.25 kΩ. KVL around the base-emitter loop: V_th = I_B R_th + 0.7 + (β + 1) I_B R_E, so with R_E = 1 kΩ and β = 100: I_B = (2.105 - 0.7)/(8.25k + 101k) = 12.9 μA, **I_C = 1.29 mA**, V_CE = V_CC - I_C R_C - I_E R_E = 12 - 1.29(4.7) - 1.30(1) = 4.66 V (with R_C = 4.7 kΩ). Stability design rule: R_th ≪ (β + 1) R_E (here 8.25 kΩ versus 101 kΩ), so I_C ≈ (V_th - 0.7)/R_E almost independent of β.\n- **Load line and Q-point:** plot I_C = (V_CC - V_CE)/R_C on the output characteristics; the Q-point sets the swing limits. **Thermal runaway** is prevented by emitter feedback.\n- **BJT as a switch:** choose I_B for forced saturation: to switch I_C = 20 mA with a forced β of 10, I_B = 2 mA and R_B = (5 - 0.7)/2 mA = 2.15 kΩ (use 2.2 kΩ); add a diode across relay coils.\n\n### 3.3 Small-signal model and amplifiers\nAt a bias I_C: **g_m = I_C/V_T**, **r_π = β/g_m**, **r_o = V_A/I_C**. For I_C = 1.29 mA: g_m = 49.8 mS and r_π = 2.0 kΩ. The hybrid-π model: i_c = g_m v_be (a voltage-controlled current source), with input resistance r_π.\n- **Common-emitter (CE) amplifier** (emitter bypassed): **A_v = -g_m (R_C ∥ R_L ∥ r_o)**, large gain with phase inversion, R_in = R_th ∥ r_π, R_out ≈ R_C. For R_C = 4.7 kΩ, R_L = 10 kΩ: A_v = -49.8 mS × 3.2 kΩ = -159.\n- **CE with an unbypassed emitter resistor:** A_v ≈ -R_C/R_E (here -4.7): much lower gain but stable and linear (negative feedback); R_in increases to about r_π + (β + 1) R_E = 103 kΩ.\n- **Common-collector (emitter follower):** A_v ≈ 1 (slightly less), high input resistance, low output resistance (r_e + R_s/(β + 1)): a **buffer**. **Common-base:** A_v ≈ g_m R_C, low R_in, good high-frequency performance.\n- **Capacitors:** coupling capacitors block dc; a bypass capacitor across R_E restores the gain at signal frequencies. The low-frequency cut-off from a coupling capacitor is f_L = 1/(2π R C): 13 Hz for 12 kΩ and 1 μF.\n- **Multistage amplifiers:** gains multiply (and add in dB); loading between stages must be included.\n\n## Chapter 4. The MOS Field-Effect Transistor (MOSFET)\n\n### 4.1 Structure and I-V characteristics\nAn **n-channel enhancement MOSFET** has a gate separated by an oxide from a p-type body; a positive gate voltage above the **threshold voltage V_t** (about 0.5 to 1 V) induces an n-type channel between source and drain. The gate draws no dc current (insulated): I_G = 0. Let V_ov = V_GS - V_t (the overdrive voltage).\n- **Cutoff:** V_GS < V_t: I_D = 0.\n- **Triode (linear) region:** V_DS < V_ov: I_D = k_n[(V_GS - V_t) V_DS - V_DS²/2] where k_n = μ_n C_ox (W/L). For small V_DS the device is a voltage-controlled resistor r_ds = 1/(k_n V_ov): for k_n = 2 mA/V² and V_GS = 5 V, V_t = 1 V: 125 Ω.\n- **Saturation (active) region:** V_DS ≥ V_ov: **I_D = (1/2) k_n V_ov² (1 + λ V_DS)**: the drain current is set by V_GS (a square law). For k_n = 2 mA/V², V_GS = 3 V, V_t = 1 V: I_D = 4 mA; with V_DD = 10 V and R_D = 1 kΩ: V_DS = 6 V ≥ V_ov = 2 V, so the device is in saturation.\n- **p-channel (PMOS)**: all polarities reversed (V_GS, V_t, V_DS negative). **Depletion-mode** and JFET devices are other kinds. **Body effect** shifts V_t. **Channel-length modulation** gives r_o = 1/(λ I_D). Scaling (Moore's law) and short-channel effects (velocity saturation, leakage).\n\n### 4.2 DC bias\n- **Gate voltage divider with a source resistor:** V_G = V_DD R_2/(R_1 + R_2) = 3.33 V (R_1 = 2 MΩ, R_2 = 1 MΩ, V_DD = 10 V); with R_S = 1 kΩ solve I_D = (1/2) k_n (V_G - I_D R_S - V_t)²: I_D = 1.23 mA and V_S = 1.23 V. Using R_S stabilises the bias against variations in k_n and V_t.\n- **Current mirror:** a diode-connected MOSFET sets V_GS and a matched transistor copies the current: I_out = I_ref (W/L)_2/(W/L)_1 in saturation; the basic building block of analog ICs (biasing and active loads).\n\n### 4.3 Small-signal model and amplifiers\n**g_m = k_n V_ov = 2 I_D/V_ov** (4 mS for the 4 mA example); **r_o = 1/(λ I_D)**.\n- **Common-source (CS) amplifier:** **A_v = -g_m (R_D ∥ R_L ∥ r_o)** = -4 mS × 1 kΩ = -4 (R_L = ∞), and -2 with R_L = 1 kΩ; R_in = R_G (very high); R_out ≈ R_D. With an unbypassed source resistor A_v ≈ -g_m R_D/(1 + g_m R_S).\n- **Source follower (common-drain):** A_v = g_m R/(1 + g_m R) < 1; very high input resistance and low output resistance (≈ 1/g_m): a buffer. **Common-gate** for high-frequency use. Compared with BJTs, MOSFETs have a lower g_m for the same current but infinite dc input resistance and are easy to integrate.\n\n### 4.4 The MOSFET as a switch and CMOS logic\nIn digital circuits the transistors act as switches (cutoff or triode). **CMOS inverter:** a PMOS pull-up and an NMOS pull-down driven by the same input: V_out = V_DD when V_in = 0 and V_out = 0 when V_in = V_DD; there is no static current path (only leakage), so **static power is almost zero**. The transfer curve has a sharp transition at the threshold V_M ≈ V_DD/2 for balanced devices. **Noise margins:** NM_H = V_OH - V_IH, NM_L = V_IL - V_OL. **Dynamic power:** **P = α C V_DD² f** (switching activity α, load capacitance C): for α = 0.1, C = 1 nF, V_DD = 1 V and f = 2 GHz: 0.2 W. **Delay** t_p ≈ 0.69 R_on C_L. **NAND** gates have series NMOS and parallel PMOS; **NOR** gates the reverse. Transmission gates, pass-transistor logic, and the DRAM and SRAM cells are also MOS circuits.\n\n## Chapter 5. Amplifier Concepts and Frequency Response\n\n- **Amplifier models:** voltage amplifier (R_in, A_vo, R_out): v_o = A_vo v_i R_L/(R_L + R_out) (loading); gain in decibels: A_v(dB) = 20 log10|A_v| (-159 is 44 dB); power gain 10 log10 P_out/P_in; cascade by multiplying gains.\n- **Frequency response:** low-frequency cut-off from coupling and bypass capacitors; high-frequency cut-off from transistor capacitances (C_π, C_μ for BJTs; C_gs, C_gd for MOSFETs). The **Miller effect** multiplies the feedback capacitance by (1 - A_v): C_gd = 2 pF with A_v = -10 gives an input capacitance contribution of 22 pF, which limits the bandwidth of high-gain stages. The **gain-bandwidth product** of a single-pole amplifier is constant (op-amp GBW).\n- **Differential pair:** two matched transistors sharing a tail current; it amplifies the difference v_1 - v_2 (differential gain A_d = g_m R_D for a MOS pair) and rejects the common-mode signal (CMRR); it is the input stage of every op-amp. A real op-amp has finite gain and bandwidth, offset voltage, bias current, slew rate and output swing limits.\n- **Distortion and linearity:** small-signal analysis is valid for signals smaller than about 10 mV for a BJT (V_T) and a fraction of V_ov for a MOSFET. **Class A, B, AB** power amplifiers differ in conduction angle (efficiency 25 percent, 78.5 percent, and between).\n\n## Chapter 6. Practical Matters\n- **Datasheets:** absolute maximum ratings (V_CEO, I_C(max), P_D), typical and minimum β (h_FE), V_CE(sat), V_th, R_DS(on), capacitances, thermal resistance.\n- **Thermal design:** junction temperature T_J = T_A + P_D θ_JA; dissipating 2 W with θ_JA = 50 °C/W at 25 °C ambient gives T_J = 125 °C (the usual limit is 150 °C); heat sinks lower θ.\n- **Simulation:** SPICE (LTspice, ngspice) for DC operating point, transient and AC analysis; always check the simulator against hand calculations.\n- **Safety and ESD:** MOS gates are destroyed by electrostatic discharge; use grounding and protection diodes.\n\n## Chapter 7. Common Mistakes Checklist\n1. Using the 0.7 V model for a diode that is in fact reverse-biased, and not checking the assumed state afterwards.\n2. Confusing DC and small-signal analysis: capacitors are open for DC and short (at mid-band) for AC; DC sources become ground in the small-signal circuit.\n3. Using r_π = V_T/I_C instead of β V_T/I_C, or computing g_m from the wrong bias current.\n4. Applying the saturation equation without verifying V_DS ≥ V_ov (or V_CE > V_CE(sat) for the BJT).\n5. Forgetting the sign of the gain (inversion) and the loading by R_L or by the next stage.\n6. Taking β as a precise number in a design: bias circuits must tolerate its variation.\n7. Neglecting the direction conventions of pnp and p-channel devices.\n8. Ignoring power and temperature limits.\n","plan":"# Electronics I (Semiconductor Devices): 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 11 hours per week (3 reading, 5 to 6 problems and simulation, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Electronics is learned by **building and measuring**: buy or borrow a breadboard, a multimeter, a few resistors, diodes, an NPN transistor (2N3904) and an N-MOSFET (2N7000 or BS170), and install LTspice or ngspice. For every circuit in the notes: calculate by hand, simulate, and (if you can) build it.\n\n## Textbooks\n- Adel Sedra and Kenneth Smith (with Tony Chan Carusone and Vincent Gaudet), *Microelectronic Circuits*. The standard text.\n- Behzad Razavi, *Fundamentals of Microelectronics*. Clear and intuition-driven.\n- Paul Horowitz and Winfield Hill, *The Art of Electronics*. The practical engineer's reference.\n- Neamen, *Microelectronics: Circuit Analysis and Design*; Millman and Halkias for device physics.\n- Ben Streetman and Sanjay Banerjee, *Solid State Electronic Devices* (for the physics of the junction and the MOS structure).\n\n## Free courses and tools\n- MIT OpenCourseWare 6.002 Circuits and Electronics and 6.012 Microelectronic Devices and Circuits.\n- Prof. Jim Hall's videos and \"Electronics Tutorials\" (electronics-tutorials.ws); All About Circuits Volume III.\n- LTspice (free), ngspice, Falstad's circuit simulator (browser), KiCad for schematics.\n- Datasheets from manufacturers (practise reading 1N4148, 1N4007, 2N3904, 2N7000, LM7805).\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Semiconductor physics (Ch 1.1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1. Checkpoint: explain doping, majority and minority carriers, and the mass-action law.\n\n**Week 2: The pn junction and the diode equation (Ch 1.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q2. Plot the diode I-V curve and its temperature dependence in a simulator.\n\n**Week 3: Diode models and circuits (Ch 2.1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q3. Solve the same circuit with the exponential and constant-drop models and compare.\n\n**Week 4: Rectifiers, regulators, special diodes (Ch 2.2, 2.3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q4 to Q6. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week.\n\n**Week 5: BJT operation and models (Ch 3.1).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1. Measure a 2N3904's β with a multimeter.\n\n**Week 6: BJT DC bias and the switch (Ch 3.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q2 to Q5. Build a fixed-bias and a voltage-divider-bias circuit and compare their sensitivity.\n\n**Week 7: BJT small-signal amplifiers (Ch 3.3).** Do: Assignment 2 Q6, Q7. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam.\n\n**Week 8: MOSFET characteristics (Ch 4.1).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1. Plot I_D against V_DS and V_GS for an NMOS in a simulator.\n\n**Week 9: MOSFET bias and amplifiers (Ch 4.2, 4.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q2, Q3.\n\n**Week 10: CMOS inverter and logic, power (Ch 4.4).** Do: Assignment 3 Q4. Simulate the voltage transfer characteristic and the transient delay.\n\n**Week 11: Frequency response, Miller effect (Ch 5).** Do: Assignment 3 Q5. Obtain Bode plots from an AC simulation.\n\n**Week 12: Differential pairs, op-amp imperfections, current mirrors (Ch 4.2, 5).** Do: Assignment 3 Q6.\n\n**Week 13: Thermal design, datasheets, design choices (Ch 6).** Do: Assignment 3 Q7, Q8. Design a small amplifier from a specification and verify it in simulation.\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo missed Class Test and assignment questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, formula sheet only).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can explain carriers, doping, the mass-action law, and the pn junction under bias.\n- [ ] I can solve diode circuits with the exponential, constant-drop and small-signal models.\n- [ ] I can design and analyse rectifiers, ripple filters and Zener regulators.\n- [ ] I can find the Q-point of BJT and MOSFET circuits and check the region of operation.\n- [ ] I can use the BJT as a switch and size the base resistor.\n- [ ] I can derive g_m, r_π, r_o and compute gain, R_in and R_out of CE, CS, CC and CD stages.\n- [ ] I can explain CMOS inverter operation, noise margins and dynamic power.\n- [ ] I can find cut-off frequencies from coupling capacitors and the Miller effect.\n- [ ] I can read a datasheet and do a basic thermal calculation.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with a formula sheet only.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. Always state the assumed region of operation and verify it after solving.\n2. Separate the DC (bias) analysis from the AC (small-signal) analysis, and draw both circuits.\n3. Use round numbers and estimates first (I_C ≈ (V_th - 0.7)/R_E) and verify with the full equations.\n4. Simulate every design and compare the simulation with your hand calculation; explain any difference.\n5. Keep a table of the formulas for g_m, r_π, r_o, gains and input/output resistances for each configuration.\n","test":"# Electronics I (Semiconductor Devices): Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each). Use V_T = 25 mV, V_BE = 0.7 V and a 0.7 V diode drop unless stated.\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** In n-type silicon the majority carriers are\nA) holes  B) electrons  C) ions  D) neutrons\n\n**2.** The mass-action law states that in equilibrium\nA) n + p = n_i  B) n p = n_i²  C) n = p always  D) n p = N_D\n\n**3.** The forward voltage drop of a silicon pn diode is about\nA) 0.2 V  B) 0.7 V  C) 1.5 V  D) 5 V\n\n**4.** Increasing the reverse bias of a pn junction\nA) narrows the depletion region  B) widens the depletion region  C) removes the depletion region  D) forward-biases it\n\n**5.** In a full-wave bridge rectifier, during each half-cycle\nA) one diode conducts  B) two diodes conduct  C) all four conduct  D) none conducts\n\n**6.** Increasing the filter capacitor of a rectifier\nA) increases the ripple  B) decreases the ripple  C) has no effect  D) lowers the frequency\n\n**7.** A Zener diode in a voltage regulator operates\nA) forward-biased  B) in reverse breakdown  C) at zero bias  D) as an open circuit always\n\n**8.** An npn BJT in the active region has\nA) BE forward-biased and BC reverse-biased  B) both junctions forward-biased  C) both reverse-biased  D) BE reverse and BC forward\n\n**9.** In the active region the collector current is\nA) β I_B  B) I_B/β  C) V_BE/R  D) zero\n\n**10.** The BJT transconductance g_m at I_C = 1 mA (V_T = 25 mV) is\nA) 4 mS  B) 40 mS  C) 100 mS  D) 1 S\n\n**11.** A common-emitter amplifier produces\nA) no phase change  B) a 180° phase inversion  C) a 90° phase shift  D) a gain of exactly 1\n\n**12.** The voltage gain of an emitter follower is about\nA) -100  B) 0  C) 1  D) 100\n\n**13.** An NMOS transistor is in saturation when\nA) V_DS < V_GS - V_t  B) V_DS ≥ V_GS - V_t and V_GS > V_t  C) V_GS < V_t  D) V_DS = 0\n\n**14.** The dc gate current of a MOSFET is\nA) large  B) equal to I_D  C) essentially zero  D) equal to I_S\n\n**15.** The channel of an enhancement NMOS forms when\nA) V_GS > V_t  B) V_GS < 0  C) V_DS = 0  D) V_GS = V_t - 1\n\n**16.** The static power consumption of a CMOS inverter is\nA) large  B) approximately zero (leakage only)  C) equal to the dynamic power  D) infinite\n\n**17.** The Miller effect\nA) reduces the input capacitance  B) multiplies the feedback capacitance by (1 + |A_v|), increasing the input capacitance  C) removes the poles  D) only affects diodes\n\n**18.** For a single-pole amplifier the gain-bandwidth product is\nA) constant  B) proportional to gain  C) zero  D) infinite\n\n**19.** A high CMRR in a differential amplifier means\nA) it amplifies common-mode signals  B) it rejects common-mode signals compared with differential ones  C) a large offset  D) low gain\n\n**20.** The junction temperature is T_J =\nA) T_A + P θ_JA  B) T_A - P θ_JA  C) P/θ_JA  D) θ_JA/P\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (10 marks)** (a) Find the hole concentration in n-type silicon with N_D = 10^16 cm^-3 (n_i = 1.5 × 10^10 cm^-3) [3]. (b) A diode with I_S = 10^-14 A: find the current at 0.65 V (V_T = 25.85 mV) [2]. (c) A 9 V supply, a 1.8 kΩ resistor and a silicon diode in series: find the current and the resistor power (0.7 V model) [3]. (d) A silicon diode's forward voltage changes by about -2 mV/°C: find the change for +40 °C [2].\n\n**B2. (10 marks)** A Zener regulator uses V_in = 15 V, a 9.1 V Zener and a load that draws up to 20 mA. The minimum Zener current for regulation is 5 mA. (a) Find the largest series resistor [3]. (b) Choose the standard value 220 Ω and find the Zener current at full load and at no load [4]. (c) Find the power in the Zener at no load [2]. (d) What is the effect of an input voltage drop to 11 V? [1]\n\n**B3. (10 marks)** A BJT with β = 150 has I_B = 20 μA, V_CC = 10 V and R_C = 2 kΩ. (a) Find I_C and V_CE [3]. (b) Find g_m and r_π at this bias [3]. (c) Estimate the gain of a CE stage with an R_L of 2 kΩ and a bypassed emitter [2]. (d) Is the transistor in the active region? Justify [2].\n\n**B4. (10 marks)** An NMOS has V_t = 0.7 V and k_n = 1 mA/V² (I_D = (1/2) k_n (V_GS - V_t)² in saturation). (a) Find I_D for V_GS = 2.7 V and V_DS = 4 V [3]. (b) Find g_m [2]. (c) With V_DD = 10 V and R_D = 2 kΩ find V_DS and check the region [3]. (d) Give the dynamic power of a CMOS gate with α = 0.2, C = 500 pF, V = 1.2 V and f = 1 GHz [2].\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **B** · 2 **B** · 3 **B** · 4 **B** · 5 **B** · 6 **B** · 7 **B** · 8 **A** · 9 **A** · 10 **B** · 11 **B** · 12 **C** · 13 **B** · 14 **C** · 15 **A** · 16 **B** · 17 **B** · 18 **A** · 19 **B** · 20 **A**\n\nBrief reasons: 10. g_m = I_C/V_T = 1 mA/25 mV = 40 mS. 16. Only leakage flows when the input is static.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) Step 1: mass action: p = n_i²/N_D = (1.5 × 10^10)²/10^16 = 2.25 × 10^20/10^16 = **2.25 × 10^4 cm^-3**.\n(b) Step 1: 0.65/0.02585 = 25.15 and e^25.15 = 8.32 × 10^10.\nStep 2: I = 10^-14 × 8.32 × 10^10 = **0.83 mA**.\n(c) Step 1: I = (9 - 0.7)/1.8 kΩ = 8.3/1800 = **4.61 mA**.\nStep 2: P_R = I² R = (4.61 mA)² × 1.8 kΩ = **38 mW**.\n(d) ΔV = -2 mV/°C × 40 °C = **-80 mV** (0.7 V falls to about 0.62 V).\n\n**B2.**\n(a) Step 1: at full load the series resistor carries I_L + I_Z,min = 20 + 5 = 25 mA.\nStep 2: R_s ≤ (V_in - V_Z)/25 mA = (15 - 9.1)/25 mA = 5.9/0.025 = **236 Ω**.\n(b) Step 1: with 220 Ω: I_Rs = 5.9/220 = 26.8 mA.\nStep 2: full load: I_Z = 26.8 - 20 = **6.8 mA** (above 5 mA: regulating).\nStep 3: no load: I_Z = **26.8 mA**.\n(c) P_Z = V_Z I_Z = 9.1 V × 26.8 mA = **244 mW** (use a 0.5 W Zener).\n(d) Step 1: at V_in = 11 V: I_Rs = (11 - 9.1)/220 = 8.6 mA.\nStep 2: this cannot supply a 20 mA load plus the Zener: **regulation is lost** (the Zener turns off and the output falls).\n\n**B3.**\n(a) Step 1: I_C = β I_B = 150 × 20 μA = **3 mA**.\nStep 2: V_CE = V_CC - I_C R_C = 10 - 3 mA × 2 kΩ = **4 V**.\n(b) Step 1: g_m = I_C/V_T = 3 mA/25 mV = **120 mS**.\nStep 2: r_π = β/g_m = 150/0.12 = **1.25 kΩ**.\n(c) A_v = -g_m (R_C ∥ R_L) = -0.12 S × 1 kΩ = **-120** (R_C ∥ R_L = 1 kΩ from the question).\n(d) Yes: V_CE = 4 V ≫ 0.2 V (not saturated) and V_BE = 0.7 V (the base-emitter junction is forward-biased, the collector is reverse-biased since V_C = 4 V > V_B = 0.7 V): **active**.\n\n**B4.**\n(a) Step 1: V_ov = V_GS - V_t = 2.7 - 0.7 = 2 V.\nStep 2: V_DS = 4 V ≥ 2 V: saturation.\nStep 3: I_D = 0.5 k_n V_ov² = 0.5 × 1 mA/V² × 4 V² = **2 mA**.\n(b) g_m = k_n V_ov = 1 mA/V² × 2 V = **2 mS** (= 2 I_D/V_ov).\n(c) V_DS = 10 - 2 mA × 2 kΩ = **6 V** ≥ V_ov = 2 V: **saturation confirmed**.\n(d) P = α C V² f = 0.2 × 500 × 10^-12 × 1.44 × 10^9 = 0.2 × 500 × 10^-12 = 10^-10; × 1.44 × 10^9 = **0.144 W**.\n"},mcq:[{"q":"In n-type silicon the majority carriers are","options":["holes","electrons","ions","neutrons"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The mass-action law states that in equilibrium","options":["n + p = n_i","n p = n_i²","n = p always","n p = N_D"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The forward voltage drop of a silicon pn diode is about","options":["0.2 V","0.7 V","1.5 V","5 V"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Increasing the reverse bias of a pn junction","options":["narrows the depletion region","widens the depletion region","removes the depletion region","forward-biases it"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a full-wave bridge rectifier, during each half-cycle","options":["one diode conducts","two diodes conduct","all four conduct","none conducts"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Increasing the filter capacitor of a rectifier","options":["increases the ripple","decreases the ripple","has no effect","lowers the frequency"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A Zener diode in a voltage regulator operates","options":["forward-biased","in reverse breakdown","at zero bias","as an open circuit always"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"An npn BJT in the active region has","options":["BE forward-biased and BC reverse-biased","both junctions forward-biased","both reverse-biased","BE reverse and BC forward"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In the active region the collector current is","options":["β I_B","I_B/β","V_BE/R","zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The BJT transconductance g_m at I_C = 1 mA (V_T = 25 mV) is","options":["4 mS","40 mS","100 mS","1 S"],"answer":1,"explain":"g_m = I_C/V_T = 1 mA/25 mV = 40 mS.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A common-emitter amplifier produces","options":["no phase change","a 180° phase inversion","a 90° phase shift","a gain of exactly 1"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The voltage gain of an emitter follower is about","options":["-100","0","1","100"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"An NMOS transistor is in saturation when","options":["V_DS < V_GS - V_t","V_DS ≥ V_GS - V_t and V_GS > V_t","V_GS < V_t","V_DS = 0"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The dc gate current of a MOSFET is","options":["large","equal to I_D","essentially zero","equal to I_S"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The channel of an enhancement NMOS forms when","options":["V_GS > V_t","V_GS < 0","V_DS = 0","V_GS = V_t - 1"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The static power consumption of a CMOS inverter is","options":["large","approximately zero (leakage only)","equal to the dynamic power","infinite"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only leakage flows when the input is static.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The Miller effect","options":["reduces the input capacitance","multiplies the feedback capacitance by (1 + |A_v|), increasing the input capacitance","removes the poles","only affects diodes"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"For a single-pole amplifier the gain-bandwidth product is","options":["constant","proportional to gain","zero","infinite"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A high CMRR in a differential amplifier means","options":["it amplifies common-mode signals","it rejects common-mode signals compared with differential ones","a large offset","low gain"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The junction temperature is T_J =","options":["T_A + P θ_JA","T_A - P θ_JA","P/θ_JA","θ_JA/P"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Doping silicon with phosphorus produces","options":["p-type material","n-type material","an insulator","intrinsic material"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The reverse saturation current of a diode roughly doubles every","options":["1 °C","5 to 10 °C","50 °C","never"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The depletion region of a pn junction under forward bias","options":["widens","narrows","disappears and the diode breaks down","is unchanged"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For a bridge rectifier from a transformer with peak secondary voltage 20 V and silicon diodes, the peak output is","options":["20 V","19.3 V","18.6 V","10 V"],"answer":2,"explain":"20 - 1.4 = 18.6 V.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In a capacitor-filtered full-wave rectifier the ripple frequency (50 Hz mains) is","options":["25 Hz","50 Hz","100 Hz","200 Hz"],"answer":2,"explain":"A full-wave rectifier doubles the frequency.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A Zener diode regulates the output voltage because in breakdown","options":["the current is constant","the voltage stays nearly constant while the current changes","it is an open circuit","it conducts only for forward bias"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The current gain β of a BJT is the ratio","options":["I_E/I_B","I_C/I_B","I_C/I_E","I_B/I_C"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A BJT saturates when","options":["both junctions are forward-biased","both are reverse-biased","only BE is reverse-biased","V_CE is very large"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A BJT bias circuit is insensitive to β if","options":["R_th ≪ (β + 1) R_E","R_th ≫ (β + 1) R_E","R_E = 0","R_C = 0"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The small-signal input resistance r_π of a BJT equals","options":["V_T/I_C","β/g_m","V_A/I_C","β V_T"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Early voltage V_A describes","options":["the supply voltage","the finite output resistance r_o = V_A/I_C","the base current","the breakdown voltage"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The input resistance of a common-collector stage is","options":["very low","high","zero","equal to R_C"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"An NMOS transistor with V_GS = 3 V, V_t = 1 V and V_DS = 0.5 V is in the","options":["cutoff region","triode region","saturation region","breakdown region"],"answer":1,"explain":"V_ov = 2 V and V_DS = 0.5 V < V_ov.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The saturation drain current of a MOSFET is proportional to","options":["V_ov","V_ov²","1/V_ov","V_DS"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The transconductance of a MOSFET in saturation is g_m =","options":["I_D/V_ov","2 I_D/V_ov","I_D V_ov","V_ov/I_D"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In a CMOS NAND gate the NMOS transistors are","options":["in parallel","in series","absent","replaced by resistors"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Dynamic power in CMOS is proportional to","options":["V_DD","V_DD²","1/V_DD","independent of V_DD"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The bandwidth of an op-amp circuit with a gain-bandwidth product of 1 MHz and a closed-loop gain of 10 is","options":["10 kHz","100 kHz","1 MHz","10 MHz"],"answer":1,"explain":"1 MHz/10 = 100 kHz.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A current mirror copies a reference current using","options":["two transistors with the same V_GS","a resistor only","a capacitor","a diode only"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The junction temperature of a device dissipating P with thermal resistance θ_JA is","options":["T_A + P/θ_JA","T_A + P θ_JA","T_A - P θ_JA","P θ_JA only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
